# Systemd preset overrides for the Azure Linux WSL variant.
#
# WSL manages networking, DNS resolution and the console on behalf of the
# distribution, so units that duplicate that management should not be enabled.
# Three sources drive this list, and they do not fully overlap:
#
#   1. The WSL systemd recommendations:
#      https://learn.microsoft.com/windows/wsl/build-custom-distro#systemd-recommendations
#   2. DISCOURAGED_SYSTEM_UNITS in WSL's distribution validator,
#      https://github.com/microsoft/WSL/blob/master/distributions/validate-modern.py
#      which runs as the "Validate tar based distributions changes" check when
#      the image is submitted for listing. It reports warnings, not errors.
#   3. Review feedback from the WSL maintainers on microsoft/WSL#41120, which
#      additionally called out the console units by name on the grounds that
#      they fail when several distributions claim the console in parallel.
#
# systemd reads preset files from all preset directories in lexicographic
# filename order and applies the first line that matches a unit, so this file
# takes precedence over both Azure Linux's 90-default.preset and systemd's own
# 90-systemd.preset. The 80- prefix matches the convention fedora-release uses
# for its variant presets (80-server.preset, 80-workstation.preset, ...).
#
# Only units with an [Install] section can be turned off from a preset.
# systemd-vconsole-setup.service, tmp.mount and the systemd-tmpfiles-setup-dev*
# units are static, so azurelinux-release-wsl masks those instead. Keep the two
# lists in sync with the comment in azurelinux-release.spec.

# WSL configures the network adapters; the distribution must not.
disable systemd-networkd.service
disable systemd-networkd.socket
disable systemd-networkd-varlink.socket
disable systemd-networkd-wait-online.service

# WSL owns DNS resolution and writes /etc/resolv.conf itself.
disable systemd-resolved.service
disable systemd-resolved-monitor.socket
disable systemd-resolved-varlink.socket

# WSL runs the distribution as a container, so systemd-getty-generator takes its
# run_container() path: it pulls in console-getty.service and never instantiates
# getty@tty1. console-getty.service gates on /dev/console, which WSL does
# provide, so this is the console unit that actually starts.
disable console-getty.service

# getty@tty1 gates on /dev/tty0 and is not instantiated under WSL, so disabling
# it only suppresses a skipped-condition entry in the journal. It is disabled
# because the WSL maintainers asked for it by name when reviewing the
# distribution listing (microsoft/WSL#41120). The glob also covers the
# getty@.service template itself, which systemd's preset enables with
# DefaultInstance=tty1.
disable getty@*.service
